{"tid":2443,"title":"Adding Java to your Path.","fid":22,"partial":false,"preserved":10,"expected":10,"last_date":"2009-09-02T05:34:00","crumbs":[{"name":"General","fid":3},{"name":"Gaming","fid":22}],"users":{"Craigsan":{"name":"Craigsan","color":"red","bold":true,"title":"Server Admin","joined":"Jun 2009","posts":241,"avatar":"2407.jpg"},"Elkinda Adnikle":{"name":"Elkinda Adnikle","color":null,"bold":true,"title":"\"I lost my third wife in 'Nam\"","joined":"Mar 2009","posts":434,"avatar":null},"Gazzy":{"name":"Gazzy","color":"red","bold":true,"title":"Server Admin","joined":"Feb 2009","posts":1081,"avatar":"1894.jpg"},"Deamie":{"name":"Deamie","color":"purple","bold":true,"title":"Super Donator","joined":"Nov 2008","posts":383,"avatar":null},"syff":{"name":"Syff","color":"red","bold":true,"title":"AsdASdaSDAsDasdaSdASdaSDAsD","joined":"Mar 2009","posts":814,"avatar":null},"Clown":{"name":"Clown","color":null,"bold":true,"title":"'Live life, have fun.'","joined":"Nov 2008","posts":2573,"avatar":null},"KiZai":{"name":"KiZai","color":null,"bold":true,"title":"Veteran","joined":"Nov 2008","posts":1523,"avatar":null},"-bug":{"name":"-Bug","color":"red","bold":true,"title":"Server Admin","joined":"Dec 2008","posts":2325,"avatar":null}},"posts":[{"pid":29548,"num":1,"date":"2009-09-01T21:54:00","author":"Craigsan","usertitle":"Server Admin","joined":"Jun 2009","author_posts":241,"title":null,"edited":null,"html":"<blockquote>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tJava is not set up properly on X64 operating systems and needs to be added to a command line in order to launch the client prompt.<br/>\n<br/>\nTo start with locate your Java directory. It should look pretty much EXACTLY like this: <b>C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Java\\jre6\\bin</b><br/>\n<br/>\n<img alt=\"\" border=\"0\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20110417004942im_/http://i708.photobucket.com/albums/ww87/Craigsan/X64%20Fix/Directory.png\"/><br/>\n<br/>\nThen you have to access your enviromental variables you do this by right-clicking \"My Computer\" and click properties then on the left hand side it should say \"Advanced System Settings\", head there.<br/>\n<br/>\n<img alt=\"\" border=\"0\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20110417004942im_/http://i708.photobucket.com/albums/ww87/Craigsan/X64%20Fix/Computerproperties.png\"/><br/>\n<br/>\nLast but not least click on the Advanced tab and then click Enviromental Variables, from there search for the Path variable as shown below.<br/>\n<br/>\n<img alt=\"\" border=\"0\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20110417004942im_/http://i708.photobucket.com/albums/ww87/Craigsan/X64%20Fix/Pathdirectory.png\"/><br/>\n<br/>\nSimply add the Java directory to the end however seperate the previous and new variables with a <b>SEMI-COLON</b>. Hit ok, ok, ok etc etc and try and re-launch the desired Java client. If it dosen't work try restarting your PC (I had to whereas others didn't) and various java related bugs and programs should work. <br/>\n<br/>\nThe large pictures are there for those who have a little more trouble grasping simple tasks ;D<br/>\n<br/>\n</blockquote>","sig":"<div><div align=\"center\"><img alt=\"\" border=\"0\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20110417004942im_/http://www.ishots-cave.com/ventmob/signatures/generated/4245999653.png\"/></div></div>"},{"pid":29549,"num":2,"date":"2009-09-01T21:55:00","author":"Elkinda Adnikle","usertitle":"\"I lost my third wife in 'Nam\"","joined":"Mar 2009","author_posts":381,"title":null,"edited":null,"html":"<blockquote>\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tHAAAAAAAAAX!\r\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</blockquote>","sig":"<div><img alt=\"\" border=\"0\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20110417004942im_/http://i40.tinypic.com/awyrn4.jpg\"/><br/>\n<img alt=\"\" border=\"0\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpoli